  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>C3.S2 — Parameter counting and the limits of universality</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<div style="background:#f8fafc;border:1px solid #e2e8f0;border-left:5px solid #026573;border-radius:8px;padding:16px 22px;margin-bottom:14px;"><p style="color:#026573;font-family:Georgia,'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:12.5px;font-weight:bold;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;margin:0;">C3.S2 — Why almost every unitary is hard</p></div><p>Consider unitaries on \(n={@n@}\) qubits.</p><p>(a) Give the number of real parameters of \(SU(2^{n})\), i.e. of an \(n\)-qubit unitary modulo global phase, as a function of <code>n</code> in general.</p><p>parameters \(=\) [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]]</p><p>(b) Evaluate that expression for \(n={@n@}\).</p><p>value \(=\) [[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]]</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>(a) \(U(d)\) is a real Lie group of dimension \(d^{2}\); quotienting the global phase \(U(1)\) leaves \(d^{2}-1\). With \(d=2^{n}\) this is \(4^{n}-1\).</p><p>(b) For \(n={@n@}\): \(4^{ {@n@} }-1={@ta2@}\).</p><p>A circuit of \(g\) gates from a fixed finite set is specified by \(O(g\log g)\) bits, so reaching all of \(SU(2^n)\) to constant accuracy requires \(g=\Omega(4^{n}/n)\). Universality therefore never implies efficiency: quantum speed-ups exist only for structured problems.</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
