  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>C3.S3 — Clifford conjugation of Pauli operators</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<div style="background:#f8fafc;border:1px solid #e2e8f0;border-left:5px solid #026573;border-radius:8px;padding:16px 22px;margin-bottom:14px;"><p style="color:#026573;font-family:Georgia,'Times New Roman',serif;font-size:12.5px;font-weight:bold;letter-spacing:2px;text-transform:uppercase;margin:0;">C3.S3 — Heisenberg picture for Clifford gates</p></div><p>Recall \(H=\frac{1}{\sqrt2}\begin{pmatrix}1&1\\1&-1\end{pmatrix}\), \(Y=\begin{pmatrix}0&-i\\i&0\end{pmatrix}\).</p><p>(a) Give the matrix \(HYH\).</p><p>\(HYH=\) [[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]]</p><p>Enter it in the form <code>matrix([a,b],[c,d])</code>.</p><p>(b) Give the matrix \(SXS^{\dagger}\) where \(S=\mathrm{diag}(1,i)\).</p><p>\(SXS^{\dagger}=\) [[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]]</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>(a) \(HXH=Z\), \(HZH=X\) and, since \(Y=iXZ\), \(HYH=iHXH\cdot HZH=iZX=-Y\). Explicitly \(HYH=\begin{pmatrix}0&i\\-i&0\end{pmatrix}\).</p><p>(b) \(SXS^{\dagger}=\begin{pmatrix}0&-i\\i&0\end{pmatrix}=Y\).</p><p>Both results illustrate the defining property of the Clifford group: conjugation maps the Pauli group to itself. Tracking Paulis instead of state vectors is the Heisenberg picture behind the Gottesman–Knill theorem and the stabilizer codes of Chapter 9 — and it is why Clifford circuits are classically simulable.</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
